WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.483 --> 00:00:01.640
Can I see your ID?

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.170
Yeah, I think I have it.

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.600
Narrator: This might be
the most famous fake ID

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.310
in any movie --

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.900
so famous that someone tried to use it

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.140
as a fake ID in real life.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.780
That's why prop makers
have to be extra careful

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.090
when making fake IDs
for movies and TV shows.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.130
Too good, and they could
get into legal trouble.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.550
We stopped by The Hand Prop Room in LA,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.310
which made the McLovin ID,

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.400
and the studio of graphic
props artist Ross MacDonald

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.880
to see how Hollywood makes its prop IDs.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.930
When creating fake IDs for production,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.060
agencies might limit their
customers to major studios.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.320
It's the prop maker's job

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.400
to make these IDs look as
real as possible on screen

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.470
without crossing into
any legal gray areas.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.580
Like with this New
Mexico driver's license,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.180
which The Hand Prop Room
made for "Black Widow."

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.670
The photo of Scarlett Johansson

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.020
has been swapped in for a stock photo,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.760
and the hologram, which
in real IDs would be 3D,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.370
is actually 2D.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.060
Jacob Kubon: There's a lot
of tricks that you can use

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.780
in the graphic itself

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.580
to make it look like it's catching light

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.200
and having some shine to it.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.510
Narrator: For a special close-up,

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.797
they might simulate
the hologram physically

00:01:07.797 --> 00:01:10.280
using a silk screen and iridescent powders

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.200
mixed with clear ink.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.120
Luckily, according to graphic
props artists Ross MacDonald,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.260
simplifying elements like
holograms and shadow images

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.700
can make the ID look better on screen,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.708
since it won't be as
jammed with tiny details.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.508
Ross: The rule of thumb is

00:01:25.508 --> 00:01:27.733
convincing is better than accurate.

00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:28.720
Narrator: To nail the aesthetics,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.680
prop makers often focus
on the sheen of the ID.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.021
Jacob: A lot of the
driver's licenses you see,

00:01:34.021 --> 00:01:36.121
especially now, have this kind of matte,

00:01:36.121 --> 00:01:37.930
but also a bit of a sheen look.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:38.960
And that's what we're going to get

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.800
from the lamination process.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.220
Narrator: Laminating will also give it

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.330
the weight of an actual driver's license,

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.030
so that when the actor holds the card,

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.280
it not only looks, but
feels like the real deal.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.940
For projects that call for close-ups,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.010
they use this old-school embossing machine

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.460
to make sure the lighting reflects

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.890
off the numbers on the
card in the right way.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.970
And for special forms of ID,
like those for the US Army,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.640
the prop makers might
have to mimic metal chips.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.580
For these, prop artists can
make fake chips out of vinyl

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.270
to stick on top of the ID,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.720
or they can print it on a card

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.001
that already has the fake chip built in.

00:02:11.001 --> 00:02:13.600
But specialized IDs pose another problem.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.080
They're often very, very plain.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.280
Military ID cards, police identification,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.870
even the FBI identification,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.960
they're meant to be
looked at really close up.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.820
But on a TV screen, they might
be sort of holding it up,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.310
and we're looking at them
from eight feet away.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:28.170
Narrator: As a prop artist,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.930
you'd maybe spice things
up with a flashy gold badge

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.120
or this type of giant FBI logo

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.490
you see popping up a
lot in shows and movies.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.087
And a lot of times the frame of reference

00:02:37.087 --> 00:02:39.210
that viewers have is other shows.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.518
The "X-Files" one is one
that you see copied a lot.

00:02:43.518 --> 00:02:46.271
Narrator: The ID also needs
to play well in the scene.

00:02:46.271 --> 00:02:49.904
FBI.

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:51.820
Narrator: Like this 1920s one Ross made

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.320
for a Prohibition agent
in "Boardwalk Empire."

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.620
He first produced an ID

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.810
faithful to what agents
carried at the time.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.010
But after some rehearsals,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.030
the filmmakers asked him to
add space for a badge inside,

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.650
so the agents would
have something to flash.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.020
Things get tricky when
it comes to passports,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.050
which are generally much
harder to forge than ID cards

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.540
because of all the detail
involved across multiple pages.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.200
To make a current US passport,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.860
The Hand Prop Room has to
print and trim the pages,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.170
do a clear overlay on the photo page,

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.120
and silk-screen the cover to
give it that authentic sheen.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.620
After sewing the pages together,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.330
the prop makers assemble the
passport and do a final trim.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.610
To avoid legal troubles,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.600
prop houses will sometimes
deliberately alter the look,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.640
like on this Netherlands
passport for "Madam Secretary."

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.800
The show's legal team
asked The Hand Prop Room

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.690
to make the passport less accurate

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.310
by reshuffling the layout and
changing the color scheme.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:47.140
With historical IDs,
legality isn't an issue,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.580
so prop artists can get
a lot more authentic

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.820
on period shows.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.830
That's what Ross did when
he made this 1920s passport

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.903
for Steve Buscemi's character
on "Boardwalk Empire."

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.430
Agent: Passport?

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.630
Narrator: Ross first had to
track down a 1921 passport

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:02.690
to use as a model,

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.180
then digitally clear out the existing info

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.070
and recreate the background pattern

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.250
so that he could go in and
write the new material on top.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.530
He had to find the red
legal seal in Europe,

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.950
since the US doesn't
use that kind anymore,

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.860
then emboss the seal and photo,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.670
make, print, and perforate
the stick-on stamps,

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.640
and recreate all the rubber stamps

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.080
to match those of the time period.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.470
He was lucky to have an
example to work with.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.920
It can be a lot harder to
find reference material

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.990
for more mundane forms of ID,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.170
like a driver's license from
a specific year and state.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.980
Ross: Especially from pre-1980,

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.020
there's no reason for
anyone to digitize that.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.753
Narrator: For this one,
the closest reference

00:04:41.753 --> 00:04:43.645
Ross could find was a photo

00:04:43.645 --> 00:04:46.680
of a New Jersey license from 1923.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.220
He scanned it and built
all this type on top of it

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.320
in the computer to recreate
a high-res version.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.600
Since this stuff is so hard
to find, Ross keeps folders

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.670
of all the reference
materials he's compiled

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.150
through the 100-plus
movies he's worked on.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.800
When he's working off a
pixelated photo like this one,

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.450
the hardest part is recreating

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.930
the actual document part of the ID.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.200
Ross has to identify all the typeface,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.770
so he places different
fonts over the original text

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.760
to find ones that match.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.960
It helps to have a
library of antique fonts

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.810
used in different time periods.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:21.930
You can date this particular
cutting of this font

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.210
to between 1815 and roughly 1825.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.518
Narrator: Sometimes,
though, you have to recreate

00:05:27.518 --> 00:05:29.310
a whole alphabet.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.670
For this 1960 passport made
for an unreleased project,

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.890
Jacob scanned six or seven
real passports from the era

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.840
to capture every letter of the alphabet,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.260
then cleaned up the
letters and vectorized them

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.910
to make a whole new font

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.660
just for this particular
year of passports.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.730
His team also had to recreate by hand

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:50.110
the blue-to-red effect of
this special '60s stamp.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.340
Replicating the look of old printing tools

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.540
takes some creative problem solving.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.550
The Hand Prop Room used printing tricks

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.663
on this '67 California license

00:05:58.663 --> 00:06:00.910
for Chris Pine's character
in "I Am the Night"

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.140
to make the text look like
it came out of a typewriter.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.740
Whenever possible, it
helps to use the real deal.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.470
Ross prints all kinds
of seals and typefaces

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.130
on his old-school letterpress,

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.410
which is operated by a foot pedal.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.770
Then there's the question
of what to write.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.680
Ross made these Illinois licenses

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.550
for a scene where the police
raid a packed speakeasy,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.670
which meant a bunch of
background characters

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.250
needed to show ID.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.060
So Ross had to make up a
bunch of names, addresses,

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.750
exact heights, weights, and more.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.233
After about the 10th one, you're like,

00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:34.420
did I use John Smith already?

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:36.440
Narrator: Ross will often
refer to period yearbooks

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.385
for names that sound old-school.

00:06:38.385 --> 00:06:40.087
Ross: This guy's Aloysius Sawicki.

00:06:40.087 --> 00:06:41.510
Narrator: And for main characters,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.560
all this info needs to be
confirmed with production.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.963
These are passport instructions

00:06:45.963 --> 00:06:48.200
for Owen Sleater in "Boardwalk Empire,"

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.925
providing details like the character's

00:06:49.925 --> 00:06:51.433
full name and hair color

00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:53.930
but leaving out other
pertinent information.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:55.320
A lot of other documentation

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.650
requires things like birth dates.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.970
You know, the writers
have never even considered

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.440
how old the character is
or what his birthday is.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:03.400
Narrator: At the end of the day, though,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.660
graphic prop artists like Ross and Jacob

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.795
don't always know how
the prop will be used

00:07:07.795 --> 00:07:08.890
when they're creating it.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.760
Sometimes these props are
highlighted in the action,

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.899
but sometimes you're doing
a lot of research and work

00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:17.607
for an ID that may only
be flashed on screen

00:07:17.607 --> 00:07:19.000
for a split second.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.330
Ross: So, incredibly elaborate prop,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.740
which we see in the episode like this.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.310
He just hands it across the counter.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.940
There were no pictures of the criminals

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.890
on wanted posters in the 19th century.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.060
It's something that comes from the movies.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.253
Most people didn't have
photographs of themselves.

